PREFLIGHT
{"file":"HKF-118.pdf","title":"Adaptive Partition and Resource Allocation for Collaborative Inference Services in UAV Swarm","page":2,"section":"II.E PPO-Based Online Orchestration","quote":"To solve the high-dimensional and strongly coupled decision problem in dynamic multi-UAV collaborative inference, we adopt Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO)."}
{"id":"HKF-011","kind":"technical","key":"t2_decisions_objective_solver","status":"PARTIAL","finding":"OP minimizes end-to-end latency and transmit power over UAV positions and binary layer allocation (C1–C9); offline P2 uses CVX with Guropi solver, online uses A3C-based multi-agent meta-RL (Algorithm 1); Table 3 parameters are legible, but exact second-action move count is on unattached p. 9.","evidence":[{"page":6,"section":"IV / Eq. (3)","quote":"The objective function in OP aims to find the UAVs' optimal positions that allocated layers that minimize the end-to-end latency and minimize the transmit powers while ensuring reliable data transmission between UAVs"},{"page":8,"section":"V / V.B","quote":"P2 is a nonlinear programming problem that can be efficiently solved using CVX with the Guropi solver, thus giving optimal layer-UAV allocations among the available UAVs."},{"page":8,"section":"V.B Deep Reinforcement Learning-Based Meta-RL Solution","quote":"Specifically, we employ a multi-agent meta-RL approach based on the asynchronous advantage actor-critic (A3C) algorithm to tackle environmental dynamics and achieve fast convergence with higher accuracy."},{"page":11,"section":"VI.B / TABLE 3","quote":"h0: 10^-5; Discount factor: 0.85; Learning rate alpha: 0.0001; Pmax: 200 mW; sigma^2: -170 dBm; Bu,v: 50 MHz; rho^th: 2 Mbps"}],"note":"HKF-011-selected-pages.pdf (orig pp. 6, 8, 10, 11). Exact discrete movement action count is cut off at the bottom of p. 8 ("The second action") and continues on unattached original p. 9."}
{"id":"HKF-011","kind":"technical","key":"t3_network_event_response","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"Models rate floor C8 (2 Mbps), energy threshold C9 (depleted UAV delegates mission and returns to central station), Table 3 radio constants (200 mW, -170 dBm, 50 MHz), and simulated training-time swarm-size (5->8->6 UAVs) and memory (560->256->512 MB) changes; no measured physical outage or mobility recovery.","evidence":[{"page":6,"section":"IV / Constraint C9","quote":"The C9 constraint in Eq. (3i) indicates that the UAV needs to have sufficient energy to process the assigned layer; otherwise, the mission will be delegated to the next UAV for execution, and the UAV with depleted energy returns to the central station by the safe energy return."},{"page":11,"section":"VI.B / TABLE 3","quote":"Pmax: 200 mW; sigma^2: -170 dBm; Bu,v: 50 MHz; rho^th: 2 Mbps; vu: 30 m/s"},{"page":12,"section":"VI.B.5 Effect of Environment Changes / Fig. 7","quote":"We investigate a practical scenario involving changes in the number of UAVs joining and disconnecting from the swarm during the learning process"}],"note":"HKF-011-selected-pages.pdf (orig pp. 6, 11, 12). Depleted-energy delegation and Fig. 7 join/leave (5->8->6 UAVs) and memory swaps are simulation-only during learning, distinct from measured physical outage or aerial mobility recovery."}
{"id":"HKF-013","kind":"appraisal","key":"D07","status":"PARTIAL","finding":"PARTIAL: Simulation models heterogeneous embedded profiles (Raspberry Pi 3B+, Orange Pi Zero, Odroid) across data rates, but assumes constant per-request data rate and all-to-all reachability, explicitly deferring dynamic networks to future RL work; no physical flight or wireless testbed.","evidence":[{"page":6,"section":"IV / Simulation Setup","quote":"In order to facilitate the calculation, we simplify the transmission model and the data rate ρi,j is constant in a complete collaborative inference request. Finally, we assume that all devices are not isolated and they can all reach each others [23]."},{"page":7,"section":"V. Conclusion","quote":"One promising future work would be using reinforcement learning as an alternative optimization scheme to predict and solve the collaborative inference problem in UAV swarm under dynamic networks."}],"note":"HKF-013-selected-pages.txt (orig pp. 6, 7). Text-only attachment; visual plots in Figs. 3–5 cannot be confirmed directly, though prose explicitly documents the static-link simulation scope."}
{"id":"HKF-028","kind":"appraisal","key":"D07","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"NO: Evaluates on a simplified 20m×20m 2D plane (1m×1m grids) using a lab GPU server (Intel Core i7-14700K, two RTX 4090 GPUs) and external standardized TTFT/TPOT benchmarks [3]; no physical UAV flight, onboard embedded execution, or measured wireless links.","evidence":[{"page":14,"section":"6.1 Experiment settings","quote":"We simplify the EC rescue scenario as a 20m x 20m plane, which is divided into 1m x 1m square grids."},{"page":14,"section":"6.1 Experiment settings","quote":"All experiments were conducted on our lab server equipped with an Intel Core i7-14700K CPU and two NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4090 GPUs."},{"page":8,"section":"3.4 LLM Inference Model","quote":"By using TTFT and TPOT metrics reported on standardized platforms, we can ensure the reproducibility of the experimental while eliminating potential deviations caused by hardware differences."}],"note":"HKF-028-selected-pages.pdf (orig pp. 8, 14). Conclusively establishes server-based grid simulation rather than physical UAV navigation validation."}
{"id":"HKF-028","kind":"outcome","key":"Q01","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"Fig. 4 prints TTFT (blue bar) labels of 17/25 s (0.68 s) for DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B (cloud model) and 19/100 s (0.19 s) for LLaMA-3.2-3B (edge model); §3.4 attributes standardized TTFT/TPOT values to external platform [3].","evidence":[{"page":15,"section":"Fig. 4","quote":"TTFT: DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B = 17/25; LLaMA-3.2-3B = 19/100; Y-axis: Time(s)"},{"page":16,"section":"6.2 Performance Comparison of Different LLMs","quote":"Based on the above comparison of different LLMs, we select DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B and LLaMA-3.2-3B as the cloud-side and edge-side foundation models, respectively, for subsequent experiments."},{"page":8,"section":"3.4 LLM Inference Model","quote":"Researchers can easily test and obtain the values of t_m^TTFT and t_m^TPOT from industry-standard platforms to evaluate different LLMs [3]."}],"note":"HKF-028-selected-pages.pdf (orig pp. 8, 15, 16). Visually verified printed fractional labels directly above blue TTFT bars in Fig. 4."}
{"id":"HKF-028","kind":"outcome","key":"Q02","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"Fig. 4 prints orange bar labels of 1/53 s for DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B and 1/246 s for LLaMA-3.2-3B; legend prints TPOT, caption prints TPOP, and p. 16 text prints TPFT; §3.4 cites external standardized platforms [3], separate from p. 14 RTX 4090 server setup.","evidence":[{"page":15,"section":"Fig. 4","quote":"TPOT: DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B = 1/53; LLaMA-3.2-3B = 1/246; Fig. 4. The TTFT and TPOP of different LLM models."},{"page":16,"section":"6.2 Performance Comparison of Different LLMs","quote":"Fig. 4 illustrates the TTFT and TPFT of different LLM models."},{"page":8,"section":"3.4 LLM Inference Model","quote":"Researchers can easily test and obtain the values of t_m^TTFT and t_m^TPOT from industry-standard platforms to evaluate different LLMs [3]."}],"note":"HKF-028-selected-pages.pdf (orig pp. 8, 14, 15, 16). Preserves source terminology inconsistency (TPOT/TPOP/TPFT) and external-benchmark provenance."}
{"id":"HKF-028","kind":"report_coverage","key":"coverage","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"VERIFIED_EXACT: Fig. 4 (p. 15) prints exact fractional labels above both TTFT and TPOT bars for all seven named LLMs, mapping directly to selected cloud/edge models (p. 16); unlabeled curves and bars in Figs. 5, 6, 9, 10, and 11 (pp. 16, 18) remain plot-only.","evidence":[{"page":15,"section":"Fig. 4","quote":"LLaMA-3.1-8B (17/100, 1/1170), Qwen2.5-7B (51/100, 1/219), Mistral-7B (17/100, 1/181), DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B (17/25, 1/53), LLaMA-3.2-3B (19/100, 1/246), LLaMA-3.2-1B (23/100, 1/272), Qwen2.5-3B (101/100, 1/103)"},{"page":18,"section":"Figs. 9–11","quote":"Fig. 10. The comparison of the performance of path planning based on LLM methods. Fig. 11. The comparison of path planning performance of different methods."}],"note":"HKF-028-selected-pages.pdf (orig pp. 15, 16, 18). Retained Q01/Q02 outcomes are exact printed labels in Fig. 4; other figures lack printed data labels."}
{"id":"HKF-029","kind":"appraisal","key":"D03","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"NO: Section 5 explicitly omits the PPO model architecture, training parameters, training procedures, and per-scenario prompts due to space constraints, preventing exact reconstruction; reward weights are not separately enumerated as an omission in that passage.","evidence":[{"page":5,"section":"5 Conclusion","quote":"Due to space constraints, detailed descriptions of the PPO model architecture, training parameters, training procedures, and the prompts in each scenario are omitted."}],"note":"HKF-029-selected-pages.txt (orig pp. 4, 5). Text-only attachment; explicit omission statement on p. 5 directly establishes non-reproducibility."}
{"id":"HKF-029","kind":"appraisal","key":"D07","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"NO: Experiments run solely in Microsoft AirSim and MATLAB on a desktop workstation (Intel Core i5-13600KF, 32 GB DDR4, RTX 3090Ti); no physical flight tests or measured aerial wireless links are reported, and real-world validation is deferred to future work.","evidence":[{"page":4,"section":"4 Experience","quote":"This platform has Intel Core i5-13600KF processor, 32 GB DDR4 memory, NVIDIA RTX 3090Ti GPU, and a 3 TB solid-state drive."},{"page":4,"section":"4 Experience","quote":"Comparative experiments on path planning were primarily implemented in MATLAB. The simulation environment was based on Microsoft AirSim"},{"page":5,"section":"5 Conclusion","quote":"In the future work, we plan to validate these capabilities in real-world scenarios"}],"note":"HKF-029-selected-pages.txt (orig pp. 4, 5). Text-only attachment; figures cannot be visually inspected, but text confirms simulation-only validation."}
{"id":"HKF-032","kind":"appraisal","key":"D06","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"NO: Trace-driven simulations plot single-point utility curves across 4–8 inference requests (Fig. 2, 5 UAVs) and 4–8 UAVs (Fig. 3, 7 requests) and point averages in Table 1 (5 UAVs, 7 requests), with no dispersion metrics, error bars, or statistical tests.","evidence":[{"page":5,"section":"Fig. 2","quote":"Number of Inference Requests: 4, 5, 6, 7, 8; Fig. 2. Utility performance under different numbers of inference requests."},{"page":6,"section":"Table 1 / V.B Results Analysis","quote":"Table 1. Latency and accuracy comparsion of different algorithms. (a) VGG16 and AlexNet: DMS Average Latency (s) 22.67, Average Accuracy (%) 87.73"},{"page":6,"section":"V.B Results Analysis","quote":"To provide a clearer comparison of the performance of the five algorithms, we have created Table. 1, which displays the latency and accuracy for a swarm of five UAVs under seven inference request scenario."}],"note":"HKF-032-selected-pages.pdf (orig pp. 5, 6). Visually confirms Fig. 2 x-axis is 4–8 (not 1–8) and confirms absence of error bars, standard deviations, or confidence intervals."}
{"id":"HKF-035","kind":"appraisal","key":"D01","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"YES: Clearly defines the UAV Extreme Edge Computing (UXEC) roles (Service Requester, Worker, Orchestrator), spatial frame decomposition with 20-pixel overlap zones, dynamic thread allocation, and result aggregation.","evidence":[{"page":2,"section":"II.A Overview","quote":"Within this ecosystem, each UAV can assume one of three principal roles: Service Requester, Worker, or Orchestrator."},{"page":2,"section":"II.B Spatial Frame Decomposition","quote":"Overlap regions of 20 pixels are maintained at partition boundaries to prevent detection fragmentation."}],"note":"HKF-035-selected-pages.pdf (orig p. 2)."}
{"id":"HKF-035","kind":"appraisal","key":"D02","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"PARTIAL: Clearly specifies spatial grid rules (1 full, 2 horizontal bisection, 3 trisection, >=4 near-square grid), 20-pixel overlap, and HTTP/JSON pipeline, but provides no formal mathematical optimization model, wireless channel equations, or battery/energy model.","evidence":[{"page":2,"section":"II.B Spatial Frame Decomposition","quote":"Single workers receive the complete frame. Two workers trigger horizontal bisection. Three workers create horizontal trisection. Four or more workers employ a grid layout computed to approximate square regions."},{"page":3,"section":"III.C Communication Protocol","quote":"Frame regions are transmitted from the orchestrator to workers as JPEG compressed images via HTTP multipart requests."}],"note":"HKF-035-selected-pages.pdf (orig pp. 2, 3). Architecture is clear but lacks formal analytical equations."}
{"id":"HKF-035","kind":"appraisal","key":"D03","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"PARTIAL: Specifies Python aiohttp, ONNX Runtime, threadpoolctl, YOLO11m (640×640, 80 COCO classes), AMD Ryzen 9 7900X (24 logical cores, 4.7 GHz), 2GB RAM/worker, and 1920×1080 30 FPS video, but omits code link, exact video dataset source, JPEG quality, and NMS/IoU thresholds.","evidence":[{"page":3,"section":"III.B Model Selection","quote":"We employ YOLO11m as the detection model, representing a practical balance between accuracy and computational efficiency for UAV applications. The model is converted to ONNX format for deployment"},{"page":3,"section":"III.D Experimental Configuration","quote":"Experiments were conducted on a workstation equipped with an AMD Ryzen 9 7900X processor providing 24 logical cores at 4.7 GHz base frequency."},{"page":3,"section":"III.D Experimental Configuration","quote":"Memory allocation was standardized at 2GB per worker to reflect realistic UAV constraints. Test video consists of urban surveillance footage at 1920 by 1080 resolution and 30 Frames Per Second (FPS)"}],"note":"HKF-035-selected-pages.pdf (orig p. 3)."}
{"id":"HKF-035","kind":"appraisal","key":"D04","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"PARTIAL: Compares distributed 2–6 worker setups (4 threads/worker) against a single-worker baseline across 2–16 threads (Table I), but includes no external collaborative-inference or layer-partitioning baselines from prior literature.","evidence":[{"page":3,"section":"IV.A Single Worker Baseline","quote":"We first establish baseline performance with a single worker processing complete frames. Table I presents inference latency across different thread allocations."},{"page":3,"section":"IV.B Distributed Inference Scalability","quote":"Figure 1 presents throughput scaling with increasing worker count, where each worker is allocated 4 threads."}],"note":"HKF-035-selected-pages.pdf (orig p. 3)."}
{"id":"HKF-035","kind":"appraisal","key":"D05","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"YES: Evaluates throughput (FPS), scaling efficiency (%), latency CDFs (median and 95th percentile in ms), heterogeneous thread allocations (Table II), dynamic thread reallocation (4->2 threads, Fig. 3), detection Precision/Recall/F1 (Table III), and per-stage time breakdown (Table IV).","evidence":[{"page":4,"section":"IV.C Latency Distribution Analysis","quote":"The four worker configuration achieves median latency of 42 milliseconds with 95th percentile at 58 milliseconds."},{"page":4,"section":"IV.E Dynamic Thread Adaptation","quote":"System throughput decreases from 22.4 to 18.6 FPS following the change, reflecting the reduced capacity of the affected worker."},{"page":4,"section":"IV.F Detection Quality Preservation / TABLE III","quote":"Single Worker: Precision 0.847, Recall 0.812, F1 Score 0.829; 4 Workers: Precision 0.843, Recall 0.806, F1 Score 0.824"}],"note":"HKF-035-selected-pages.pdf (orig pp. 4, 5)."}
{"id":"HKF-035","kind":"appraisal","key":"D06","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"PARTIAL: Reports empirical latency CDFs (Fig. 2) with median and 95th percentiles and a 15 s throughput time series (Fig. 3), but omits total frame count, number of trials, standard deviations/confidence intervals in Tables I–IV, and statistical significance tests.","evidence":[{"page":4,"section":"IV.C Latency Distribution Analysis","quote":"Two worker distribution compresses the latency distribution substantially, with 95th percentile latency reduced from 178 milliseconds to 89 milliseconds."},{"page":4,"section":"IV.D / TABLE II","quote":"Homogeneous (4,4,4,4): Latency 44 ms, FPS 22.4; Mild Heterogeneity (6,4,4,2): Latency 52 ms, FPS 19.2; Strong Heterogeneity (8,4,2,2): Latency 67 ms, FPS 14.9"}],"note":"HKF-035-selected-pages.pdf (orig p. 4)."}
{"id":"HKF-035","kind":"appraisal","key":"D07","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"NO: Simulates UAV workers as local processes on one AMD Ryzen 9 7900X workstation with thread-count and 2GB caps over local HTTP; no real UAV hardware, flight dynamics, energy constraints, or wireless channel/mobility modeling.","evidence":[{"page":3,"section":"III.D Experimental Configuration","quote":"This configuration simulates a fleet of UAV workers with varying computational capabilities by allocating different thread counts to each worker process."},{"page":5,"section":"IV.G Communication Overhead Analysis","quote":"Network transfer latency is highly dependent on inter node bandwidth and would increase significantly in bandwidth constrained wireless scenarios typical of UAV deployments."}],"note":"HKF-035-selected-pages.pdf (orig pp. 3, 5)."}
{"id":"HKF-035","kind":"appraisal","key":"D08","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"PARTIAL: Quantitative conclusion claims (68% per-frame latency reduction for 4 workers vs. 1 worker, <1% precision/recall drop, ~21% distribution overhead) match Tables I–IV, but claiming feasibility at the UAV extreme edge relies solely on single-workstation process simulation.","evidence":[{"page":5,"section":"V. Conclusion","quote":"Experimental evaluation demonstrated that distributing inference across four UAV workers reduces per frame latency by 68% compared to single node processing"},{"page":5,"section":"V. Conclusion","quote":"The distribution overhead of approximately 21% represents a reasonable cost for the substantial parallelization benefits achieved"}],"note":"HKF-035-selected-pages.pdf (orig p. 5)."}
{"id":"HKF-035","kind":"appraisal","key":"D09","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"PARTIAL: Table IV quantifies 4-worker per-frame overhead (3.2 ms encoding 7.3%, 4.1 ms network transfer 9.3%, 34.8 ms inference 79.1%, 1.9 ms aggregation 4.3%; total 44.0 ms; ~21% distribution overhead), but omits power/energy consumption and wireless bandwidth modeling.","evidence":[{"page":5,"section":"IV.G / TABLE IV","quote":"Frame Encoding: 3.2 ms (7.3%); Network Transfer: 4.1 ms (9.3%); Worker Inference: 34.8 ms (79.1%); Result Aggregation: 1.9 ms (4.3%); Total: 44.0 ms (100%)"},{"page":3,"section":"III.C Communication Protocol","quote":"Frame regions are transmitted from the orchestrator to workers as JPEG compressed images via HTTP multipart requests."}],"note":"HKF-035-selected-pages.pdf (orig pp. 3, 5)."}
{"id":"HKF-035","kind":"appraisal","key":"D10","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"PARTIAL: Discloses NSERC funding (Grant RGPIN-2025-05001) and notes orchestrator bottlenecks, boundary-object confidence drops, and wireless bandwidth sensitivity, but lacks a formal limitations section, conflict-of-interest statement, and code/data availability disclosure.","evidence":[{"page":5,"section":"ACKNOWLEDGMENT","quote":"This research was supported by the Natural Sciences and Engineering Research Council of Canada (NSERC) under Grant RGPIN-2025-05001."},{"page":5,"section":"IV.G Communication Overhead Analysis","quote":"Network transfer latency is highly dependent on inter node bandwidth and would increase significantly in bandwidth constrained wireless scenarios typical of UAV deployments."}],"note":"HKF-035-selected-pages.pdf (orig p. 5)."}
{"id":"HKF-035","kind":"outcome","key":"Q01","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"Single-worker YOLO11m baseline (1920×1080 frames, Table I): 2 threads = 275 ms / 3.6 FPS; 4 threads = 145 ms / 6.9 FPS; 8 threads = 107 ms / 9.3 FPS; 12 threads = 89 ms / 11.2 FPS; 16 threads = 78 ms / 12.8 FPS.","evidence":[{"page":3,"section":"IV.A / TABLE I","quote":"Threads 2: 275 ms, 3.6 FPS; 4: 145 ms, 6.9 FPS; 8: 107 ms, 9.3 FPS; 12: 89 ms, 11.2 FPS; 16: 78 ms, 12.8 FPS"}],"note":"HKF-035-selected-pages.pdf (orig p. 3). Table I first row thread count "2" is confirmed verbatim in §IV.A prose ("With 2 threads, inference requires approximately 275 milliseconds per frame, yielding a maximum throughput of 3.6 FPS.")."}
{"id":"HKF-035","kind":"outcome","key":"Q02","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"Distributed scaling at 4 threads/worker (§IV.B, Fig. 1): 2 workers = 12.8 FPS (1.85× speedup); 3 workers = 18.1 FPS (2.62× speedup); 4 workers (2×2 grid) = 22.4 FPS (3.25× speedup, 68% per-frame latency reduction vs. 1 worker; >80% scaling efficiency through 4 workers).","evidence":[{"page":3,"section":"IV.B Distributed Inference Scalability","quote":"With 2 workers processing horizontal frame halves, aggregate throughput reaches 12.8 FPS, representing 1.85 times speedup over the single worker baseline. Three workers achieve 18.1 FPS with 2.62 times speedup. Four workers in a 2 by 2 grid configuration deliver 22.4 FPS, yielding 3.25 times speedup and representing a 68% reduction in per frame latency compared to single worker processing."}],"note":"HKF-035-selected-pages.pdf (orig p. 3). 5- and 6-worker points in Fig. 1 are plot-only."}
{"id":"HKF-035","kind":"outcome","key":"Q03","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"4-worker median/p95 latency is 42/58 ms (vs. p95 89 ms for 2 workers, 178 ms for 1 worker); Table II 4-worker latency/FPS: homogeneous 44 ms/22.4, mild heterogeneity 52 ms/19.2 (-14%), strong 67 ms/14.9 (-33%); 4->2 thread shift takes ~45 ms (22.4->18.6 FPS, 0 dropped); Table III F1: 0.829->0.824 (<1% drop).","evidence":[{"page":4,"section":"IV.C Latency Distribution Analysis","quote":"The four worker configuration achieves median latency of 42 milliseconds with 95th percentile at 58 milliseconds."},{"page":4,"section":"IV.D / TABLE II","quote":"Homogeneous (4,4,4,4): 44 ms, 22.4 FPS; Mild Heterogeneity (6,4,4,2): 52 ms, 19.2 FPS; Strong Heterogeneity (8,4,2,2): 67 ms, 14.9 FPS"},{"page":4,"section":"IV.E Dynamic Thread Adaptation","quote":"The thread change takes effect within one frame processing cycle, approximately 45 milliseconds. System throughput decreases from 22.4 to 18.6 FPS following the change"},{"page":4,"section":"IV.F / TABLE III","quote":"Single Worker: 0.847 / 0.812 / 0.829; 2 Workers: 0.845 / 0.809 / 0.827; 4 Workers: 0.843 / 0.806 / 0.824"}],"note":"HKF-035-selected-pages.pdf (orig p. 4)."}
{"id":"HKF-035","kind":"outcome","key":"Q04","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"4-worker per-frame processing breakdown (Table IV): Frame Encoding 3.2 ms (7.3%), Network Transfer 4.1 ms (9.3%), Worker Inference 34.8 ms (79.1%), Result Aggregation 1.9 ms (4.3%), Total 44.0 ms (100%); distribution overhead is approximately 21%.","evidence":[{"page":5,"section":"IV.G / TABLE IV","quote":"Frame Encoding 3.2 ms (7.3%); Network Transfer 4.1 ms (9.3%); Worker Inference 34.8 ms (79.1%); Result Aggregation 1.9 ms (4.3%); Total 44.0 ms (100%)"},{"page":5,"section":"IV.G Communication Overhead Analysis","quote":"Distribution overhead comprising encoding, transfer, and aggregation consumes approximately 21% of total processing time."}],"note":"HKF-035-selected-pages.pdf (orig p. 5)."}
{"id":"HKF-035","kind":"report_coverage","key":"coverage","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"VERIFIED_EXACT: Tables I–IV and §IV prose report exact numerical values with units for single-worker latency/FPS, 2–4 worker throughput/speedup, median/p95 latency, heterogeneity, dynamic thread adaptation, accuracy, and overhead breakdown; 5–6 worker points in Fig. 1 and full curves in Figs. 2–3 are plot-only.","evidence":[{"page":3,"section":"IV.A / TABLE I & IV.B","quote":"Table I: 275–78 ms, 3.6–12.8 FPS; Fig. 1 caption: Near linear speedup observed up to 4 workers with efficiency exceeding 80%."},{"page":5,"section":"IV.G / TABLE IV","quote":"Total 44.0 ms 100%"}],"note":"HKF-035-selected-pages.pdf (orig pp. 3–5)."}
{"id":"HKF-035","kind":"technical","key":"t2_decisions_objective_solver","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"Rule-based heuristic orchestration with no formal mathematical objective or optimization solver: orchestrator chooses spatial grid layout by worker count (1 full, 2 horizontal bisection, 3 trisection, >=4 near-square grid) and adjusts per-worker CPU threads online via threadpoolctl without model reloading.","evidence":[{"page":2,"section":"II.B Spatial Frame Decomposition","quote":"For n workers, the orchestrator computes an optimal grid arrangement that minimizes aspect ratio distortion while balancing computational load."},{"page":3,"section":"III.A System Components","quote":"The inference engine supports dynamic thread allocation through the threadpoolctl library, enabling real time adjustment of computational intensity without model reloading."},{"page":4,"section":"IV.E Dynamic Thread Adaptation","quote":"Figure 3 shows system response when a worker's thread allocation is reduced from 4 to 2 during operation"}],"note":"HKF-035-selected-pages.pdf (orig pp. 2, 3, 4)."}
{"id":"HKF-035","kind":"technical","key":"t2_partition","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"Input-space spatial frame decomposition (tile/data partitioning, not layer-wise DNN splitting): splits each video frame into non-overlapping rectangular regions with 20-pixel boundary overlap zones, runs full YOLO11m per worker, then transforms coordinates and deduplicates overlap detections via IoU thresholds.","evidence":[{"page":2,"section":"II.B Spatial Frame Decomposition","quote":"When a video frame arrives at the orchestrator, it is partitioned into non overlapping rectangular regions with small overlap zones at boundaries to ensure detection continuity."},{"page":2,"section":"II.D Result Aggregation","quote":"Detections from overlap zones undergo duplicate elimination using intersection over union thresholds to identify and merge redundant detections of objects appearing in multiple regions."}],"note":"HKF-035-selected-pages.pdf (orig p. 2)."}
{"id":"HKF-038","kind":"appraisal","key":"D07","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"NO: Abstracts aerial wireless links to static spectral efficiencies Si under a sum-bandwidth bound Bmax without UAV mobility, channel fading, or flight dynamics, and evaluates via CIFAR-10 simulations rather than physical UAV or wireless tests.","evidence":[{"page":2,"section":"II.A System Model / Eq. (7)","quote":"We denote Si as the spectrum efficiency of the transmission from UAV i to the cloud server. The total bandwidth consumed by all UAVs can not exceed the maximum available bandwidth Bmax"},{"page":1,"section":"Abstract","quote":"Simulation results on the CIFAR-10 dataset demonstrate that our approach significantly enhances system accuracy compared to frameworks for independent identically distributed data"}],"note":"HKF-038-selected-pages.txt (orig pp. 1, 2). Text-only attachment; confirms static link abstraction and simulation scope."}
{"id":"HKF-043","kind":"outcome","key":"Q01","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"Table III (60 simulated UAVs) prints Energy consumption (J) of 778.7 J, 670.8 J, and 596.1 J for energy-efficiency settings 0.6, 0.7, and 0.8, respectively.","evidence":[{"page":12,"section":"VII.B / TABLE III","quote":"Energy efficiency 0.6: Energy consumption (J) 778.7; 0.7: 670.8; 0.8: 596.1"},{"page":12,"section":"VII.B","quote":"Table III presents the sensitivity analysis results for the energy efficiency with 60 UAVs."}],"note":"HKF-043-selected-pages.pdf (orig p. 12). Visually verified directly from Table III."}
{"id":"HKF-043","kind":"outcome","key":"Q02","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"Table III (60 simulated UAVs) prints Inference latency (s) of 0.515 s, 0.511 s, and 0.513 s for energy-efficiency settings 0.6, 0.7, and 0.8, respectively.","evidence":[{"page":12,"section":"VII.B / TABLE III","quote":"Energy efficiency 0.6: Inference latency (s) 0.515; 0.7: 0.511; 0.8: 0.513"}],"note":"HKF-043-selected-pages.pdf (orig p. 12). Visually verified directly from Table III."}
{"id":"HKF-043","kind":"outcome","key":"Q03","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"Table III (60 simulated UAVs) prints Throughput (IPS) of 116.6 IPS, 117.4 IPS, and 117.1 IPS for energy-efficiency settings 0.6, 0.7, and 0.8, respectively.","evidence":[{"page":12,"section":"VII.B / TABLE III","quote":"Energy efficiency 0.6: Throughput (IPS) 116.6; 0.7: 117.4; 0.8: 117.1"}],"note":"HKF-043-selected-pages.pdf (orig p. 12). Visually verified directly from Table III."}
{"id":"HKF-043","kind":"report_coverage","key":"coverage","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"VERIFIED_EXACT: Table III (p. 12) prints exact values with units for Inference latency (s), Energy consumption (J), and Throughput (IPS) across energy-efficiency settings 0.6, 0.7, and 0.8 (60 UAVs); curves/boxplots in Figs. 3, 4, 10, and 11 remain plot-only.","evidence":[{"page":12,"section":"VII.B / TABLE III","quote":"0.6 | 0.515 | 778.7 | 116.6; 0.7 | 0.511 | 670.8 | 117.4; 0.8 | 0.513 | 596.1 | 117.1"},{"page":10,"section":"Figs. 3–4","quote":"Fig. 3. Comparison of inference latency across different algorithms and UAV scales. Fig. 4. Box plot of inference latency across different algorithms and UAV scales."}],"note":"HKF-043-selected-pages.pdf (orig pp. 10, 12)."}
{"id":"HKF-043","kind":"technical","key":"t2_decisions_objective_solver","status":"PARTIAL","finding":"Minimizes inference latency and energy over UAV deployment, offloading, bandwidth allocation, and DAG partitioning: centralized MINLP uses JDTSO (GA + DP + CVXPY + DAG partition), distributed Dec-POMDP uses MAPDP (MAPPO + bounded brute-force DAG partition); Table II (p. 9) is legible, but Eqs. (16)/(23) are on unattached pp. 6–7.","evidence":[{"page":2,"section":"I. Introduction","quote":"A two-layer optimization algorithm, called JDTSO, is employed to jointly optimize the deployment of UAVs and task scheduling. At the upper layer optimization, a genetic algorithm (GA) is presented to determine UAV positions. At the lower layer optimization, dynamic programming (DP) and a DAG partition strategy are used to obtain the exact-optimal solution."},{"page":8,"section":"VI.B Algorithm","quote":"Furthermore, to reduce the complexity of the action space, DAG partitioning is decoupled from MAPPO. And it is addressed using brute-force enumeration. Subsequently, the problem is solved using MAPPO."},{"page":9,"section":"VII.A.2 / TABLE II","quote":"We employ the CVXPY toolkit to address the communication resource allocation problem."},{"page":9,"section":"TABLE II","quote":"V: 5, 10, 20, 40, 60, 100, 200; h: 200m; Pv: 30 dBm; sigma^2: -174 dBm; Gamma_LoS: 5dB; fc: 2GHz; B_c^mm: 28GHz; P^v,hov: 60 w; phi_v: 70%; B_v^max: 20 MHz; E_v^max: 500 KJ; d_min: 10 m"}],"note":"HKF-043-selected-pages.pdf (orig pp. 2, 8, 9, 12). Section V and explicit Eqs. (16) and (23) lie on unattached original pages 6–7."}
{"id":"HKF-043","kind":"technical","key":"t3_network_event_response","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"Models static hovering altitude (200 m), deterministic LoS UAV-to-UAV path loss (5 dB, 2 GHz, 20 MHz) and 28 GHz mmWave BS links (Table II), and evaluates UAV-count scaling (5–200); trajectory planning, mobility constraints, and inter-UAV interference are deferred to future work with no link-failure resilience tested.","evidence":[{"page":5,"section":"IV.A Network Modeling","quote":"Throughout its flight, all UAVs maintains a constant altitude h, which is set to the minimum altitude to avoid collisions and reduce interference with other UAVs"},{"page":9,"section":"TABLE II","quote":"h: 200m; Pv: 30 dBm; sigma^2: -174 dBm; Gamma_LoS: 5dB; fc: 2GHz; B_c^mm: 28GHz; B_v^max: 20 MHz; d_min: 10 m"},{"page":12,"section":"VIII. Conclusion","quote":"In future work, we will consider UAV trajectory planning, mobility constraints (e.g., minimum turning radius and climb rate), and interference from other UAVs."}],"note":"HKF-043-selected-pages.pdf (orig pp. 5, 9, 10, 12)."}
{"id":"HKF-044","kind":"technical","key":"t2_decisions_objective_solver","status":"PARTIAL","finding":"Problem (P1) maximizes GTST completion rate OBJ over trajectory, communication/sensing beamforming, compute allocation, target scheduling, and satellite-controlled multi-block NN splitting, solved by CTDE collaborative MA-DDPG with zero-padding and action masking (Eqs. 25–26); p. 10 Table II parameter cells are omitted from the text extract.","evidence":[{"page":6,"section":"III.E Optimization Problem Formulation","quote":"Therefore, we need to maximize the GTST task completion rate by jointly optimizing UAV trajectory, beamforming, computation resource allocation, NN layer split and target scheduling"},{"page":7,"section":"IV Joint Optimization Scheme Based on Collaborative Multi-Agent DDPG","quote":"Our scheme is proposed based on multi-agent deep deterministic policy gradient (MA-DDPG), which adopts centralized training (CT) and decentralized execution (DE) architecture."},{"page":8,"section":"IV.B Centralized Training Algorithm With Parameters Sharing","quote":"Secondly, we implement the output dimension adaptation of the actor network by mask operation."}],"note":"HKF-044-selected-pages.txt (orig pp. 4–10). Text-only attachment (PyMuPDF 1.26.7) includes the "TABLE II SIMULATION PARAMETERS" heading on p. 10 without its cell values; visual table parameters cannot be confirmed from this text extract (parser limitation, not a source omission)."}
