{"id":"HKF-085","kind":"appraisal","key":"D02","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"PARTIAL: Formulates graph-based early-exit partitioning, delay/energy/channel equations, constraints C1–C4, and Option-Critic updates with consistent negative latency and energy signs in Eqs. (10) and (18) (no positive-energy sign typo), but omits flight energy, memory bounds, w1–w3 values, and Tmax/Emax thresholds.","evidence":[{"page":3,"section":"II.D Problem Formulation / Eq. (10)","quote":"max_{pi, ei} J(pi, ei) = -(w1 * T_total(ei) + w2 * E_total(ei)) + w3 * A(ei)"},{"page":4,"section":"III.B.3 Reward Function / Eq. (18)","quote":"Rt = -w1 T_total(ei) - w2 E_total(ei) + w3 A(ei)."},{"page":3,"section":"II.C.2 / Eq. (7)","quote":"E_exe(vk, uri) = epsilon_i a_k"},{"page":5,"section":"TABLE I","quote":"epsilon_i: 0.2 J/s"}],"note":"HKF-085-selected-pages.pdf (orig pp. 2, 3, 4, 5, 6). Visually confirms leading minus covers both latency and energy in Eq. (10) and both are subtracted in Eq. (18); also notes unit mismatch in Table I (epsilon_i = 0.2 J/s vs. per-computation-unit coefficient in Eq. 7)."}
{"id":"HKF-085","kind":"appraisal","key":"D07","status":"PARTIAL","finding":"PARTIAL: Models a path-loss/Rayleigh wireless channel (alpha=3, g0~CN(0,1), N=8 UAVs, B=20 MHz) in simulation, but evaluates a simplified single-task AlexNet setup without physical UAV hardware, mobility dynamics, or real-world communication uncertainties (explicitly deferred to future work).","evidence":[{"page":5,"section":"IV / TABLE I","quote":"N: 8; B: 20 MHz; N0: -100 dBm; PTx: 1.258 W; alpha: 3; Ci^u: evenly distributed from 1GHz to 2GHz; g0: follows the complex normal distribution CN(0, 1)"},{"page":6,"section":"IV. Performance Evaluation","quote":"In single-task scenarios, even with 20 MHz bandwidth, fewer than three of eight UAVs are utilized, leaving resources idle."},{"page":6,"section":"V. Conclusion","quote":"Nevertheless, future work should explore the impact of more complex UAV swarm dynamics and real-world communication uncertainties to further validate and extend the applicability of the proposed approach."}],"note":"HKF-085-selected-pages.pdf (orig pp. 5, 6). Preserves explicit future-work qualification on swarm dynamics and real-world channel uncertainties."}
{"id":"HKF-100","kind":"appraisal","key":"D08","status":"PARTIAL","finding":"PARTIAL: Section 6 (p. 12) claims robustness across "different UAV speeds, varying mission areas, and fluctuating task arrival rates," whereas §5/Table 2 (p. 8) reports one nominal circular speed (75 m/s, 20×20 km area) with no speed-sensitivity comparison on supplied pages and no field validation.","evidence":[{"page":12,"section":"Conclusion","quote":"Moreover, we showed that our solution is robust under a variety of operational conditions, including different UAV speeds, varying mission areas, and fluctuating task arrival rates."},{"page":8,"section":"5. Performance Evaluation / Table 2","quote":"In our environment, UAVs are deployed over a 20×20 km area and follow circular trajectories with a movement radius of 1000 m, traveling at speeds of up to 75 m/s."},{"page":8,"section":"Table 2","quote":"Area Bounds: 20x20 km; Movement Radius: 1000 m; Movement Speed: 75 m/s; Number of Runs: 50"}],"note":"HKF-100-selected-pages.txt (orig pp. 8, 12). Unattached results pages 9–11 were not supplied to confirm whether any speed-sweep plot appears there; text confirms simulation-only scope without field validation."}
{"id":"HKF-104","kind":"appraisal","key":"D08","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"NO: Two internal conflicts: Highlights/Abstract/§1 claim Orion is the only framework running 70B on UAV nodes, contradicting §4.2 where Jupiter (27,264 ms) and EdgeShard (43,058 ms) finish in simulation; full Orion 70B latency is 18,665 ms (§4.2) vs. 16,793 ms (§4.6) under consistent settings (unresolved).","evidence":[{"page":1,"section":"Highlights","quote":"and is the only framework that successfully runs a 70B-parameter LLM entirely on memory-constrained UAV onboard computers."},{"page":17,"section":"4.2 Latency on Llama-2 Models of Varying Scales","quote":"Among all successful distributed solutions, Orion remained the most efficient with an 18,665 ms latency, significantly outperforming Jupiter (27,264 ms) and EdgeShard (43,058 ms)."},{"page":19,"section":"4.6 Ablation Study","quote":"The experimental settings remained consistent with Section 4.2: a 64-token sensor input length, 1000 Mb/s inter-UAV bandwidth, and 200 Mb/s air-to-ground bandwidth."},{"page":19,"section":"4.6 Ablation Study","quote":"Specifically, for the Llama-2 70B model, the latency surged from 16,793 ms to 42,092 ms, a nearly 150% increase."},{"page":23,"section":"5.5 Real-System Deployment and Physical Validation","quote":"We plan to implement Orion on a physical UAV testbed (e.g., quadcopters equipped with NVIDIA Jetson modules) to capture real system overheads and validate robustness."}],"note":"HKF-104-selected-pages.txt (orig pp. 1, 3, 16, 17, 19, 23). Preserves both 70B figures separately without averaging and distinguishes simulated from physical execution."}
{"id":"HKF-109","kind":"appraisal","key":"D10","status":"PARTIAL","finding":"PARTIAL: Section 5 transparently qualifies that low drift for SiamAPN/AiATrack is unreliable because it is computed only on minimal successful frames and discloses HIL test modality; however, no funding, conflict-of-interest, or formal limitations sections appear on supplied pages (and success rate is 81.9% p. 1 vs. 74.8% p. 6).","evidence":[{"page":5,"section":"5.1 / Tracking Evaluation","quote":"These values are considered unreliable indicators of robustness due to both models exhibiting extremely high failure counts and low success rates, which are calculated only on a minimal subset of successful frames."},{"page":5,"section":"5.2 UAV Data Handover","quote":"The onboard system tests were performed in a hardware-in-the-loop (HIL) environment, which included visual tracking, feature matching, inter-UAV coordination, and ROS-based navigation logic, resulting in stable end-to-end performance at 4–5 Hz"},{"page":1,"section":"Abstract","quote":"achieving a tracking success rate of 81.9% during real-time flight control at 100 Hz"},{"page":6,"section":"6 Conclusion","quote":"This study introduced a UAV-based vision framework for autonomous marine animal tracking, achieving 74.8% success rate and real-time performance in dynamic marine environments."}],"note":"HKF-109-selected-pages.txt (orig pp. 1, 5, 6). Unattached pages 2–4 were not supplied; funding, COI, and formal limitations sections were not located on pp. 1, 5, 6."}
